                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1902-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Extinct Audio BM9</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Bidirectional Ribbon Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The BM9 &#8220;Viking&#8221; is the flagship ribbon microphone from Extinct Audio. It is a passive design, meaning the mic contains no circuitry to increase its output level. Like most ribbon mics, it produces a bidirectional (aka &#8220;figure of eight&#8221;) polar pattern. </p><p>This mic&#8217;s physical design is inspired by the vintage Bang &amp; Olufsen BM3. </p><p>Like most passive ribbon microphones, the BM9 has relatively low sensitivity, specified at 2.23mV/Pa. For comparison, the Shure SM57&#8217;s sensitivity is ~1.4dB lower, at 1.9mV/Pa. Typical large-diaphragm condenser mics range from 12mV/Pa to 40mV/Pa (15dB or more higher).</p><p>The ribbon material is 1.8 microns aluminum. Extinct Audio staff manufactures their ribbon motors in-house, including cutting, crimping, and tensioning ribbons.</p><p>The BM9&#8217;s stated frequency range is 30Hz-15kHz. The response curve exhibits characteristic similarities to many ribbon microphones, including the relatively soft top end (dropping ~2.5dB above 7kHz) and pronounced proximity effect. </p><p>The mic&#8217;s output transformer is wound in-house, with output impedance of 300 Ohms. Alternative windings are available by special request. </p><p>The BM9 &#8220;Viking&#8221; ships with a custom wooden box and a hard mount. The Fenrir vibration mount is available at an extra cost. </p><p>Extinct Audio also makes a coupler which replaces the domed caps on two BM9s, allowing two microphones to be locked together for Blumlein stereo recording.</p><p>Extinct warranties the BM9 for the life of the owner. The lifetime warranty excludes ribbon replacement after the first year, and excludes cosmetic wear of the mic&#8217;s exterior.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Extinct-Audio/BM9">Extinct Audio BM9</a> is also known as: Viking.</p><p>The mic was released in 2017.</p>
